      "updated_at": "2026-02-03T06:47:12Z",
      "body": "As someone who\u2019s worked in UI design and then spent the last decade in development, I wanted to share a bit of feedback.\n\nThe video is great, thoughtful and thorough. From a usability and accessibility standpoint though, I\u2019d avoid making core UI elements visible only on mouseover.\n\nHidden interactions increase user learning time, especially for new users, and break common UI expectations (like logos linking home or clearly visible navigation). Keeping the hamburger menu always visible, or placing it more explicitly, would make the interface easier to understand at a glance.\n\nMouseover and dropdown interactions are also harder to make fully accessible, particularly for keyboard, touch, or motor-impaired users. Dialogs or slide-in menus with clear close actions tend to work better here and align well with modern, cross-device design. I personally (not a contributor yet) am a fan of getting rid of dropdowns menu where possible, and would love if _everyone_ can use homarr :)\n\nOverall, I really like Homarr and the direction you\u2019re exploring. If you keep looking into usability improvements like this, I\u2019d definitely be interested in contributing a PR or two.",
      "author_association": "NONE",
